 time delay  coordinates and when we find the  sparsest model and we allow ourselves to  have nonlinear terms and linear terms  we find that the sparsest models tend to  be  linear honest-to-goodness linear models  with no quadratic terms no cubic terms  just linear and this has deep  connections it turns out to coupement  operator theory  we kind of discovered we stumbled on  this connection  that time delay coordinates essentially  provide  almost an optimal coordinate system to  getting a coupeman linear system  a sparse coupeman linear system and  we've written  you know four or five follow-on papers  exploring the structure of this  why it works how it works applying it to  chaotic systems and deterministic  systems and we've learned a lot  so i'll put some links to those papers  and  videos in in the description  and this model allows you to do things  like predict  even very chaotic dynamics um you know  so like you can predict when you're  about to switch lobes to the other side  of the lorenz attractor  and you can even color code your  attractor based on when it's essentially  acting like a linear system and when  it's about to switch  things like that so we learned about  this because we were trying to handle  cindy  on systems with latent variables and so  it turns out that time delay coordinates  plus cindy gives you a very very  accurate  koopman linear model okay there's like a  whole  many papers and an hour-long video on  this so i'm not going to  dwell on this for too long just a long  way of saying  you can handle latent measurements  oftentimes by delay embedding  and this is actually something kathleen  champion explored quite a bit  which is for non-chaotic systems you  often get a perfectly closed model  using those delay embeddings with the  cindy regression procedure  okay good so we've been talking about  this kind of big  cindy overview and specifically these  key challenges  so now we've covered challenge zero of  the data challenge one  of the coordinates and in the next  videos we're going to talk about the  library  so how do you actually build that theta  matrix so it's well conditioned  so that it describes the dynamics of  your system so that it's not too large  and what optimization algorithms do we  use to actually find  those sparse models in the first place  okay all coming up soon  thank you  you
 

